v {xschem version=3.4.8RC file_version=1.2
*
* Marks a net that leaves this block through the symbol's `extra`
* attribute rather than through a drawn pin.
*
* Put one on `ibias`, on a body tie, or on either of a tail bank's
* implicit gate/source nets. It emits a comment and nothing else -- no
* device, no port, no change to any connection.
*
* It takes no attributes. The net name it draws and the name it writes
* into the netlist are both `@#0:net_name`, read from whatever net the
* marker is attached to, so neither can disagree with the schematic.
* (It used to carry a hand-typed `lab=`, which nothing checked: a marker
* reading `lab=toplabel` on a net that netlisted as `net1` produced
* `* implicit port toplabel` with no complaint. Changed 2026-08-30.)
*
* What it is for. `extra` puts a net on the instance line and in the
* .subckt port list without giving the symbol a pin box, which is how
* this library keeps bias and body connections off your schematic. Those
* ports are invisible to xschem's connectivity check, so inside a device
* schematic a net like `ibias` looks like a transistor gate with nothing
* feeding it, and every netlist reports
*
*     Error: undriven node: ibias
*     Warning: open net: b
*
* on a design that is perfectly correct. The error is alarming and wrong:
* the port is in the .subckt line, and examples/currentsource/ measures
* its mirror at exactly ratio x ibias.
*
* Why this shape of fix. xschem has no switch for it -- the binary has
* only erc_open_net_is_error and erc_shorted_output_is_error, and neither
* covers the undriven case, which is hard-coded as an error. A label
* declared dir=out or dir=inout does not count as a driver (both tried).
* A real ipin/iopin makes xschem reject the schematic instead, since
* `extra` pins are not pin boxes. What does work is `type=noconn`, which
* tells the checker a net is deliberately left alone -- so this symbol
* borrows that type while saying the accurate thing on the sheet and in
* the netlist. Verified 2026-08-28: the reporting stops and the netlist
* gains one comment line per marker.
*
* Why it is drawn this way. A stub into an open circle is the off-sheet
* terminal shape: it says the net continues somewhere not on this
* canvas, which is what an `extra` port does. The earlier drawing ended
* in two shrinking crossbars, which is the earth-ground shape and so
* read as the net being terminated -- the opposite of the truth. The
* words "implicit port" are in this comment rather than on every
* instance, and the net name sits on layer 15 so it reads as annotation
* and not as a device.
}
G {}
K {type=noconn
format="* implicit port @#0:net_name"
template="name=e1"
}
V {}
S {}
E {}
B 5 -1.25 -1.25 1.25 1.25 {name=p dir=inout}
L 4 0 0 9 0 {}
A 4 12 0 3 270 360 {}
T {@#0:net_name} 18 -5 0 0 0.2 0.2 {layer=15}
